// Grade 9 passages (validated by live-patches/grade-journey/validate.mjs). Built by validate.mjs --write.
export const PASSAGES = [{"id":"g9-u1-p01","unit":"G9-U1","pool":"practice","title":"Should Schools Pay Students to Learn?","genre":"argument","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Imagine a school that hands out cash the way other schools hand out gold stars. A student who finishes a book earns two dollars. A student who shows up every day for a month earns twenty. To many adults, the idea sounds like bribery, and bribery seems like the opposite of education. Yet a handful of American school systems have tested exactly this idea, and the results are more interesting than either side usually admits. Paying students can work, but only if schools pay for effort rather than for results.\n\nThe best-known tests were led by Roland Fryer, an economist at Harvard University, in the late 2000s. His team gave millions of dollars to thousands of students in Chicago, Dallas, New York City and Washington, D.C. Each city tried a different design. In New York, students earned money for scoring well on tests. In Dallas, second graders earned two dollars for every book they read, as long as they passed a short quiz showing that they had actually read it. In Washington, middle school students earned money for showing up, behaving well and turning in their work.\n\nThe outcomes split along a clear line. Paying for test scores did little or nothing to raise them. Paying for actions that students could control, such as reading a book, produced real gains. The Dallas students who were paid to read did better on reading tests at the end of the year than similar students who were not paid, even though no one had paid them for their scores at all.\n\nThe reason is not hard to see once we think like a student. A fourteen-year-old who is offered fifty dollars for a higher math score may want the money badly, but wanting is not the same as knowing what to do. Should she study more? Study differently? Ask for help? A reward for a final result does not tell anyone how to reach it. A reward for reading a chapter, finishing a problem set or attending a tutoring session does. It turns a distant goal into a series of clear, small steps, and each step builds the skill that the test is supposed to measure.\n\nCritics raise a serious objection. Psychologists have long warned that rewards can crowd out a person's own interest in a task. If students learn to read for money, the argument goes, they may stop reading the moment the money stops. This concern deserves respect, and some laboratory studies do show it happening, especially when people are rewarded for something they already enjoy. But many struggling students do not enjoy reading yet. For them, the problem is not that a reward will replace their love of books. The problem is that they have never read enough to discover that love in the first place. A small payment can get a reluctant reader through the hard early stage, when every page feels slow, and into the stage where reading starts to pay for itself.\n\nA second objection is cost. Schools are short of money, and every dollar spent on rewards is a dollar not spent on teachers or books. That is true, but the sums involved are modest. A program that pays two dollars a book costs far less per student than hiring an extra tutor, and it can be aimed at the students who need it most.\n\nNone of this means that schools should start paying for every grade on every report card. The evidence points the other way. Rewards tied to outcomes ask students to solve a puzzle they have not been taught to solve. Rewards tied to effort hand them a map. If schools are willing to experiment, they should pay for the reading, the practice and the attendance, and then let the scores take care of themselves.","caption":"Rewards tested in four cities (late 2000s): New York paid for test scores; Dallas paid for books read; Washington paid for attendance, behavior and work turned in.","questions":[{"type":"claim","q":"What is the author's main claim?","options":["Schools should never use money to reward their students.","Schools that pay students should reward effort, not results.","Schools should pay students only for high test scores."],"answer":1},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the claim that paying for effort can raise achievement?","options":["This concern deserves respect, and some laboratory studies do show it happening, especially when people are rewarded for something they already enjoy.","Paying for actions that students could control, such as reading a book, produced real gains.","Schools are short of money, and every dollar spent on rewards is a dollar not spent on teachers or books."],"answer":1,"evidence":"Paying for actions that students could control, such as reading a book, produced real gains."},{"type":"claim","q":"How does the author answer the worry that rewards crowd out a student's own interest?","options":["By saying that struggling readers have not yet found that interest.","By saying that laboratory studies of rewards are always wrong.","By saying that schools should simply pay students much more."],"answer":0},{"type":"inference","q":"Why does the author describe effort-based rewards as handing students \"a map\"?","options":["They show students which subjects pay the most money.","They let students skip the steps that feel slow and hard.","They break a distant goal into clear steps students can take."],"answer":2}],"grade":9,"words":635,"auto":{"fk":6.7,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g9-u1-p02","unit":"G9-U1","pool":"practice","title":"Free Fares or Better Buses?","genre":"argument","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"In recent years, a number of cities have experimented with a bold idea: making public transit completely free. Tallinn, the capital of Estonia, began offering free rides to registered residents in 2013. Luxembourg made buses and trains free nationwide in 2020, and Kansas City, Missouri, eliminated bus fares the same year. Supporters argue that free transit is fair, simple and good for the environment. Those goals are worthy. But for most cities, the smartest investment is not free fares. It is service that is frequent and reliable enough that people actually want to use it.\n\nStart with what riders say they want. When transit agencies survey residents, the complaints that come up again and again are about waiting, not paying: buses that arrive every forty minutes, routes that stop running at eight in the evening, and trips that take twice as long as driving. A free bus that comes once an hour is still a bus most people will avoid. A bus that comes every ten minutes, on the other hand, changes how a person can plan a day. Transit planners have a saying for this: frequency is freedom.\n\nNext, consider the money. Fares do not cover the full cost of running a transit system anywhere in the United States, but in many cities they still pay for a meaningful share of daily operations. When fares disappear, that money has to be replaced, usually from the same local budget that would otherwise pay for more drivers and more buses. A city that spends its limited funds replacing fare revenue may have nothing left to improve service. In effect, it would be giving current riders a discount on a product that does not meet the needs of the people who are not riding at all.\n\nThe evidence from free-fare cities supports this caution. Ridership often rises after fares are removed, but studies of Tallinn found that many of the new trips came from people who had previously walked or biked, not from people who left their cars at home. If the goal is to reduce traffic and pollution, a policy that mainly moves pedestrians onto buses is a weak tool.\n\nDefenders of free transit make an important point in response. For low-income workers, even a two-dollar fare, paid twice a day, adds up to a serious expense over a month. Fares also slow buses down, because every rider who fumbles for change or a card adds seconds at every stop. These arguments are strong, and they deserve a practical answer rather than dismissal.\n\nThat answer is targeted help. Many cities already offer reduced fares for students, seniors and people with low incomes, and these programs can be expanded. Fare payment can be made faster with tap cards and boarding through all doors, which removes much of the delay at stops. These steps address the fairness problem directly while preserving the revenue needed to run more buses.\n\nNone of this means free fares are always wrong. In a small city where fares bring in very little money, or in a downtown zone where collecting them costs nearly as much as they raise, dropping fares can make sense. The point is about priorities. A transit system succeeds when people can count on it, and people count on a bus that shows up.\n\nSo before a city council votes to make its buses free, it should ask a simple question: if the buses were free tomorrow, would they also come more often? If the honest answer is no, the city should spend its money on service first. Free fares can wait. A bus worth riding cannot.","questions":[{"type":"claim","q":"Which statement best expresses the author's claim?","options":["Cities should usually fund frequent service before free fares.","Every city should make public transit free for all riders.","Bus fares should rise to pay for newer and cleaner buses."],"answer":0},{"type":"claim","q":"What evidence does the author use to question the effect of free fares on traffic?","options":["Kansas City riders asked for more evening routes.","Luxembourg spent more on trains than on buses.","Many new Tallinn riders had walked or biked before."],"answer":2},{"type":"inference","q":"How does the author treat the argument that fares burden low-income workers?","options":["as a strong point that calls for a targeted solution","as a weak point that the evidence fully disproves","as an unimportant detail that most riders ignore"],"answer":0},{"type":"vocab","q":"In paragraph 5, what does the word \"dismissal\" most nearly mean?","options":["refusing to take something seriously","sending someone home early from work","agreeing with every part of a claim"],"answer":0,"evidence":"These arguments are strong, and they deserve a practical answer rather than dismissal."}],"grade":9,"words":605,"auto":{"fk":7.8,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g9-u1-c01","unit":"G9-U1","pool":"check","title":"Red Should Mean Stop, Even on the Right","genre":"argument","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"For most American drivers, a red light at an intersection is only half a stop. After a brief pause, a driver who wants to turn right may roll forward and make the turn, as long as the way looks clear. The rule feels so ordinary that many people assume it has always existed. It has not. Right turn on red spread across the country in the 1970s for a reason that has little to do with safety, and in crowded cities it is time to end it.\n\nThe rule's history explains a great deal. During the oil crisis of the 1970s, Americans were urged to save fuel wherever they could. Supporters argued that cars idling at red lights wasted gasoline, and that letting them turn would keep traffic moving. A federal energy law passed in 1975 encouraged states to allow the turn by tying it to money for their energy-saving programs. By 1980, almost every state permitted right turns on red. The change was made to save fuel, not to protect the people crossing the street.\n\nThe fuel savings turned out to be small, while the risks were real. Studies in the years after the rule spread found that crashes involving right-turning cars and pedestrians rose noticeably at intersections where the turn was allowed. The danger comes from the way the turn works. A driver waiting to turn right on red is looking left, searching for a gap in oncoming traffic. Meanwhile, a person on foot may be stepping off the curb on the driver's right, in the very crosswalk the car is about to enter. The driver sees the gap, presses the gas and turns into someone he never looked at. Children, older adults and people in wheelchairs are especially hard for a distracted driver to notice.\n\nThis problem matters most in dense cities, where intersections are crowded with people walking, biking and waiting for buses. New York City has long banned right turns on red unless a sign says otherwise, and its traffic still moves. In 2022, the Council of the District of Columbia voted to ban the turn at most intersections in the nation's capital. Other cities, from Chicago to San Francisco, have debated similar steps. Their reasoning is simple: in a place where thousands of people cross the street every hour, saving a driver a few seconds is not worth putting walkers at risk.\n\nOpponents make a fair point. Without the turn, they say, lines of cars will grow longer, buses will run slower and drivers will grow frustrated. Some drivers may even run the light out of impatience. These are real concerns, but they can be managed. Cities can allow the turn at quiet intersections with good sight lines and post clear signs banning it where crowds gather. They can also give walkers a head start, a signal that lets people enter the crosswalk a few seconds before cars get a green light. The goal is not to punish drivers or to make every trip slower. It is to match the rule to the street.\n\nOthers argue that drivers should simply be more careful. Of course they should. But a rule that depends on every driver looking in two directions at once, every time, will fail sooner or later, because people make mistakes. Good traffic design accepts that fact and builds in protection, the same way guardrails protect drivers who drift on a curve.\n\nRight turn on red was a solution to a fuel shortage that ended long ago. In the busiest parts of our cities, it has outlived its purpose. When the light is red, everyone should stop and wait.","caption":"Timeline: 1975, federal energy law encourages right turn on red; by 1980, nearly every state allows it; 2022, Washington, D.C., votes to ban it at most intersections.","questions":[{"type":"claim","q":"What claim does the author make about right turns on red?","options":["They should be ended in crowded city areas.","They should be allowed at every intersection.","They should be decided by drivers themselves."],"answer":0},{"type":"author","q":"Why does the author explain the 1970s oil crisis?","options":["To show that the rule saved huge amounts of fuel.","To show that the rule was never about safety.","To show that drivers wasted gas on purpose."],"answer":1},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best explains why the turn endangers people on foot?","options":["Supporters argued that cars idling at red lights wasted gasoline, and that letting them turn would keep traffic moving.","A driver waiting to turn right on red is looking left, searching for a gap in oncoming traffic.","Without the turn, they say, lines of cars will grow longer, buses will run slower and drivers will grow frustrated."],"answer":1,"evidence":"A driver waiting to turn right on red is looking left, searching for a gap in oncoming traffic."},{"type":"claim","q":"How does the author respond to the counterclaim about longer lines of cars?","options":["By denying that traffic would slow at all.","By proposing signs, quiet-street exceptions and head starts.","By arguing that buses should be removed from city streets."],"answer":1},{"type":"craft","q":"What does the comparison to guardrails suggest?","options":["Good design should protect people from ordinary mistakes.","Drivers on curves are more careful than drivers in cities.","Traffic rules work best when they are rarely enforced."],"answer":0}],"grade":9,"words":608,"auto":{"fk":6.9,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g9-u1-c02","unit":"G9-U1","pool":"check","title":"A Floor Under Failing Grades","genre":"argument","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Picture a tenth grader named Marcus who earns a 90 on each of his first three history tests. Then his grandmother becomes seriously ill, his family spends two difficult weeks at the hospital, and he never turns in a major project. His teacher records a zero. When the four scores are averaged, Marcus drops from an A to a D, and a single missing assignment has erased most of a semester of excellent work. A growing number of high schools have decided that this arithmetic is unfair, and they have adopted what are called minimum-grade policies. Under these rules, the lowest score a teacher may record on an assignment is a 50, not a zero. Critics describe the policy as grade inflation, but it is actually one of the simplest ways to make grades more accurate.\n\nThe problem lies in the unusual shape of the traditional grading scale. On a 100-point scale, an A, a B, a C and a D each cover approximately ten points, while an F covers roughly sixty. That means a failing score can fall six times farther below passing than an A can rise above it. A zero is therefore not simply a failing grade; it is a failing grade with exaggerated force. Averaged with other scores, it pulls a student down much harder than any individual high score can lift him up. If Marcus had received a 50 instead of a zero, his average would have been an 80, a B. That result is still lower than his test scores, which is reasonable, since he did not complete the project, but it is a far more accurate description of what he actually understands.\n\nGrades are supposed to communicate how much a student has learned. When one zero transforms a student who understands the material into a student who appears to be failing, the grade stops describing learning and starts describing a terrible two weeks. Minimum-grade policies do not erase the missing work, because a 50 is still an F. They simply prevent a single F from outweighing everything else on the record.\n\nThe effect on motivation may matter even more. A student who falls far behind early in a semester can calculate her situation precisely. If several zeros have pushed her average so low that even perfect work cannot rescue it, she has every reason to stop trying. Teachers sometimes call this moment the point of no return. A floor under failing grades keeps recovery possible, and it tells students that the door remains open if they are willing to walk back through it.\n\nOpponents raise a legitimate concern. If a student can receive a 50 for doing nothing, they argue, some students will deliberately do nothing, since skipping an essay would cost less than it used to. This is a genuine risk, and schools that adopt minimum grades must address it directly. The answer is to combine the policy with firm expectations. Missing work should still be recorded as missing, reported to families and followed by an opportunity, and a requirement, to complete it. A student who collects many 50s will still fail the course. The policy changes how severely a single zero hits, not whether effort matters.\n\nOthers insist that the professional world does not award partial credit for work that was never done. That observation is accurate, but school is not a workplace. It is an institution designed for learning, and learning includes recovering from mistakes. A grading system that makes one setback permanent teaches students exactly the wrong lesson about failure.\n\nNo grading policy is perfect, and a 50 is not a gift. It is a way to keep one disastrous week from telling a false story about an entire semester. Schools that care about accurate grades should put a floor under the F.","caption":"How one score changes an average: four scores of 90, 90, 90 and 0 average out to a D; with 50 in place of the 0, they average 80, a B.","questions":[{"type":"claim","q":"What is the author's central claim about minimum-grade policies?","options":["They make grades a more accurate picture of learning.","They let students pass classes without doing any work.","They should replace letter grades with written comments."],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the claim that a zero has unusual force?","options":["A growing number of high schools have decided that this arithmetic is unfair, and they have adopted what are called minimum-grade policies.","That means a failing score can fall six times farther below passing than an A can rise above it.","Under these rules, the lowest score a teacher may record on an assignment is a 50, not a zero."],"answer":1,"evidence":"That means a failing score can fall six times farther below passing than an A can rise above it."},{"type":"author","q":"Why does the author open with the story of Marcus?","options":["To show how one zero can distort a strong record.","To prove that most students miss major projects.","To argue that teachers should excuse family illness."],"answer":0},{"type":"claim","q":"How does the author answer the concern that students will skip work?","options":["By admitting the policy has no real weaknesses.","By pairing the policy with firm expectations for missing work.","By suggesting that essays should no longer be graded."],"answer":1},{"type":"vocab","q":"In the passage, what does \"floor\" most nearly mean?","options":["a lowest allowed level","a surface to stand on","a story of a building"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Schools that care about accurate grades should put a floor under the F."}],"grade":9,"words":637,"auto":{"fk":8.8,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g9-u1-c03","unit":"G9-U1","pool":"check","title":"Is Space Worth the Money?","genre":"argument","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Every few years, someone asks why the United States spends billions of dollars sending machines and people into space when there are so many problems to solve on Earth. It is a fair question, and it deserves a careful answer. Space exploration is worth what it costs, because the investment is smaller than most people think, the practical benefits are larger, and the knowledge it produces cannot be gained any other way.\n\nConsider the size of the budget first. NASA's yearly budget is about twenty-five billion dollars. That sounds enormous, but it amounts to less than half of one percent of total federal spending. Surveys have repeatedly found that Americans guess the share is far higher, often imagining that a quarter of the budget goes to space. Even at the height of the Apollo program in the 1960s, when the nation was racing to land astronauts on the Moon, NASA received only around four percent. Cutting the space program entirely would barely change the federal budget, and it would not solve the problems critics point to.\n\nThe second reason is that space research pays off on the ground. Satellites track hurricanes days before they reach land, giving families time to evacuate. They monitor crops, measure melting ice and map wildfires. Technologies developed for space missions have found their way into daily life. Memory foam began as a NASA-funded project to cushion aircraft seats, and the camera sensors in most smartphones grew out of work at NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory in the 1990s. Not every spending program can point to such a wide range of benefits.\n\nThird, space science answers questions that matter. By studying other planets, scientists have learned how a world can lose its atmosphere, as Mars did, or overheat, as Venus did. These lessons help us understand our own planet's climate. Telescopes in orbit have detected thousands of planets around other stars, reshaping what we know about our place in the universe. Missions to asteroids and comets have revealed clues about how the solar system formed, including where Earth's water may have come from.\n\nThere is also a more personal reason. My uncle watched a rocket launch when he was ten years old, and he says it is the reason he became an engineer. Everyone I have talked to about space feels the same way. Clearly, space exploration inspires young people more than any other activity, and if we stop exploring, the next generation will stop dreaming altogether.\n\nCritics argue that the money would do more good if it were spent directly on poverty, health care or education. This concern comes from a good place, and those needs are urgent. But the choice is not truly between space and people. Because the space budget is so small, eliminating it would provide only a tiny increase for any of these causes. Meanwhile, much of the money spent on space is spent on Earth, paying the salaries of scientists, engineers and factory workers across the country.\n\nOthers claim that private companies can now handle space travel without government help. Private rockets have indeed lowered the cost of reaching orbit. Yet companies invest where they expect profits, and much basic research, such as studying distant planets or the early universe, offers no quick return. A company may build an excellent rocket, but it rarely has a reason to pay for a telescope that studies galaxies billions of light-years away. Public science programs fill that gap, just as government funding has long supported medical research that private firms would not pay for alone.\n\nSpace exploration is not a luxury that competes with solving problems on Earth. It is one of the tools we use to solve them. A nation that spends a fraction of a penny of each tax dollar on reaching beyond its own sky is making one of its wisest investments.","questions":[{"type":"claim","q":"Which reason does the author give for supporting space spending?","options":["Private companies refuse to build any rockets at all.","Its practical benefits reach people on the ground.","It is the largest part of the federal budget."],"answer":1},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the claim that the space budget is small?","options":["That sounds enormous, but it amounts to less than half of one percent of total federal spending.","Surveys have repeatedly found that Americans guess the share is far higher, often imagining that a quarter of the budget goes to space.","These lessons help us understand our own planet's climate."],"answer":0,"evidence":"That sounds enormous, but it amounts to less than half of one percent of total federal spending."},{"type":"claim","q":"Which part of the argument is the least well supported?","options":["the paragraph about satellites and technology","the paragraph about the size of the budget","the paragraph about the uncle and inspiration"],"answer":2},{"type":"inference","q":"Why is the claim that \"the next generation will stop dreaming altogether\" weak?","options":["It relies on budget numbers that are out of date.","It jumps to an extreme result without any evidence.","It ignores the views of scientists at NASA."],"answer":1},{"type":"claim","q":"How does the author answer critics who prefer spending on poverty and health?","options":["by arguing the space budget is too small to change much","by claiming that poverty has already been solved","by agreeing that the space program should end"],"answer":0}],"grade":9,"words":644,"auto":{"fk":9,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g9-u3-p01","unit":"G9-U3","pool":"practice","title":"Counting a Nation","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Every ten years, the United States tries to count every person living within its borders. The count, called the census, can seem like a dull exercise in paperwork. In fact, it is written into the Constitution, it decides how political power is divided, and the struggle to carry it out helped launch the age of the computer.\n\nThe framers of the Constitution needed a fair way to divide seats in the House of Representatives among the states. They decided that seats would be shared out according to population, and that the population would be measured by an \"actual Enumeration\" taken every ten years. Article I, Section 2 sets out this rule. The census was therefore never meant to satisfy curiosity. It was meant to answer a question of power: how many voices in Congress does each state deserve?\n\nThe first census began on August 2, 1790. There were no census offices, no printed forms in the mail and no paid army of counters waiting to begin. Instead, the job fell to federal marshals and the assistants they hired. These assistants traveled on horseback and on foot, visiting farms, villages and the young cities of the East Coast. They carried their own paper and wrote down the answers by hand. The questions were few. Counters recorded the name of the head of each household and the number of free white males sixteen and older, free white males under sixteen, free white females, all other free persons, and enslaved people. That last category reflected one of the Constitution's darkest compromises, under which an enslaved person counted as three-fifths of a person when seats were divided.\n\nThe count took about eighteen months and found roughly 3.9 million people. Both President George Washington and Secretary of State Thomas Jefferson suspected that the true number was higher. Some households had been missed, and some people had avoided the counters, perhaps because they feared that the government would use the information to raise taxes. From the very first census, then, Americans faced a problem that has never gone away: a count is only as good as the public's willingness to be counted.\n\nAs the nation grew, the census grew with it, and so did the work. Congress kept adding questions about jobs, farms, schooling and birthplace. By the 1880 census, the volume of information had become overwhelming. Clerks sorted and tallied the results largely by hand, and it took nearly eight years to finish processing them. Officials feared that the 1890 census might not be finished before the 1900 census began.\n\nThe solution came from a young engineer named Herman Hollerith. He designed a system in which each person's answers were recorded as holes punched in a stiff paper card. A machine pressed metal pins against the card. Wherever a pin passed through a hole, it touched a small cup of mercury beneath, completing an electrical circuit and advancing a counting dial. A clerk could feed in card after card, and the machine did the tallying. Using Hollerith's tabulators, the Census Office announced a population total for 1890 of about 62.6 million in a matter of weeks rather than years.\n\nHollerith's invention reached far beyond the census. He started a company to sell his machines to railroads and businesses, and through a series of mergers, that company became part of the firm later named International Business Machines, or IBM. Punched cards remained a basic tool of computing well into the 1970s.\n\nToday, the census still serves its original purpose of dividing seats in Congress, and its numbers also guide the spending of hundreds of billions of federal dollars each year. The tools have changed from horseback and quill pen to mailed forms and online questionnaires. The central challenge, reaching every person, remains the same as it was in 1790.","caption":"Census milestones: 1790, about 3.9 million people counted by hand; 1880, results take nearly eight years to process; 1890, Hollerith's punched-card machines speed the count.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"What central idea does the passage develop about the census?","options":["It began as a simple survey and soon lost its purpose.","It is a constitutional tool of power that drove new technology.","It has always counted every American without any errors."],"answer":1},{"type":"literal","q":"How did Hollerith's machine record a count from each card?","options":["A pin passed through a hole, completing a circuit.","A clerk read each card aloud to a second clerk.","A marshal punched a new card for every family."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Wherever a pin passed through a hole, it touched a small cup of mercury beneath, completing an electrical circuit and advancing a counting dial."},{"type":"why","q":"Why did officials worry about the 1890 census before Hollerith's system?","options":["Too many marshals had stopped working for the government.","Hand tallying was so slow it might overlap the next census.","Congress had decided to remove most of its questions."],"answer":1},{"type":"inference","q":"What does the passage suggest about the problem Washington and Jefferson noticed?","options":["It was solved for good by Hollerith's punched cards.","It mattered only in the years before the Constitution.","It depends on people, so technology alone cannot fix it."],"answer":2}],"grade":9,"words":634,"auto":{"fk":8.3,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g9-u3-p02","unit":"G9-U3","pool":"practice","title":"Frances Perkins and the Safety Net","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"On the afternoon of March 25, 1911, a young social worker named Frances Perkins was having tea with friends near Washington Square in New York City when she heard fire engines. She ran toward the noise and found the Triangle Waist Company factory burning. The workers on its upper floors, most of them young immigrant women, were trapped. Exit doors had been locked, the single fire escape collapsed, and the firefighters' ladders could not reach high enough. In less than half an hour, 146 workers died. Perkins watched from the street. Years later, she said that the fire had shown her, more clearly than any report, what happened when a society left workers unprotected.\n\nThe fire turned a young reformer into a determined one. Perkins went to work investigating factory conditions for New York State. She led lawmakers on tours of crowded workshops and bakeries in cellars, where they could see the dangers for themselves. Over the next several years, New York passed dozens of laws requiring fire exits, sprinklers, safer machinery and shorter hours for women and children. Perkins learned a lesson she would use for the rest of her career: people in power change their minds most quickly when they see a problem with their own eyes.\n\nIn 1929, Governor Franklin D. Roosevelt named Perkins to lead New York's labor department. When Roosevelt was elected president in 1932, he asked her to become secretary of labor. Before she accepted, Perkins told him what she would want to accomplish. Her list included unemployment insurance, pensions for the elderly, a minimum wage, limits on working hours and an end to child labor. Roosevelt agreed that she should try. In 1933, Perkins became the first woman ever to serve in a president's cabinet.\n\nShe took office during the worst years of the Great Depression. Roughly one in four American workers had no job. Many older people had lost their savings when banks failed, and most had no pension to fall back on. Families who had worked all their lives found themselves depending on charity. To Perkins, this was the factory fire on a national scale: millions of people with no exit.\n\nIn 1934, Roosevelt asked Perkins to lead the Committee on Economic Security, a small group charged with designing a national plan. The committee worked through the summer and fall, often late into the night, on questions that had never been tested in the United States. Should insurance be run by the states or the federal government? How would it be paid for? Who would be covered? Perkins pushed the committee toward a plan that workers and employers would pay into together, so that benefits would be seen as earned rather than given as charity.\n\nThe result was the Social Security Act, which Roosevelt signed on August 14, 1935. It created old-age insurance for retired workers, a joint federal and state system of unemployment insurance, and aid for needy children and people with disabilities. It did not cover everyone at first. Farm workers and household workers, for example, were left out, which excluded many Black and immigrant workers. Later laws gradually widened the system.\n\nPerkins served as secretary of labor for twelve years, longer than anyone else has held the job. In 1938, she helped win passage of the Fair Labor Standards Act, which set the first national minimum wage, limited the workweek and banned most child labor. Nearly every item on the list she showed Roosevelt in 1933 had become law. The safety net that Americans now take for granted was built, in large part, by a woman who never forgot what she saw from a New York sidewalk.","caption":"Frances Perkins: 1911, witnesses the Triangle fire; 1933, becomes the first woman in a U.S. cabinet; 1935, Social Security Act signed; 1938, Fair Labor Standards Act passes.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"What is the central idea of the passage?","options":["Perkins turned what she witnessed into lasting national laws.","Perkins mainly worked to improve fire departments in New York.","Perkins believed workers should rely on charity in hard times."],"answer":0},{"type":"sequence","q":"What did Perkins do before she agreed to become secretary of labor?","options":["Perkins told him what she would want to accomplish.","She signed the Social Security Act into law herself.","She closed the Triangle Waist Company for good."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Before she accepted, Perkins told him what she would want to accomplish."},{"type":"author","q":"Why does the author compare the Great Depression to \"the factory fire on a national scale\"?","options":["To show that many factories burned during the Depression.","To show how Perkins saw millions trapped without protection.","To show that Perkins blamed workers for losing their jobs."],"answer":1},{"type":"inference","q":"Why did Perkins want workers and employers to pay into the system?","options":["So that benefits would feel earned and not like charity.","So that the federal government would spend nothing at all.","So that only factory workers could ever receive benefits."],"answer":0}],"grade":9,"words":610,"auto":{"fk":7.7,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g9-u3-c01","unit":"G9-U3","pool":"check","title":"Is It a Crime for a Citizen to Vote?","genre":"document","source":"Susan B. Anthony, \"Is It a Crime for a Citizen of the United States to Vote?\" (1873), as printed in History of Woman Suffrage, Vol. II, ed. Elizabeth Cady Stanton, Susan B. Anthony and Matilda Joslyn Gage (1882), chapter XXV; excerpt, gaps marked […]","license":"public-domain","text":"FRIENDS AND FELLOW CITIZENS:—I stand before you to-night, under indictment for the alleged crime of having voted illegally at the last Presidential election. I shall endeavor this evening to prove to you that in voting, I not only committed no crime, but simply exercised my \"citizen's right,\" guaranteed to me and all United States citizens by the National Constitution, beyond the power of any State to deny.\n\nOur democratic republican government is based on the idea of the natural right of every individual member thereof to a voice and a vote in making and executing the laws. We assert the province of government to be to secure the people in the enjoyment of their inalienable rights. We throw to the winds the old dogma that governments can give rights. Before governments were organized, no one denies that each individual possessed the right to protect his own life, liberty, and property. And when 100 or 1,000,000 people enter into a free government, they do not barter away their natural rights; they simply pledge themselves to protect each other in the enjoyment of them, through prescribed judicial and legislative tribunals. They agree to abandon the methods of brute force in the adjustment of their differences, and adopt those of civilization. The Declaration of Independence, the National and State Constitutions, and the organic laws of the Territories, all alike propose to protect the people in the exercise of their God-given rights. Not one of them pretends to bestow rights.\n\nAll men are created equal, and endowed by their Creator with certain inalienable rights. Among these are life, liberty, and the pursuit of happiness. That to secure these, governments are instituted among men, deriving their just powers from the consent of the governed.\n\nHere is no shadow of government authority over rights, nor exclusion of any class from their full and equal enjoyment. Here is pronounced the rights of all men, and \"consequently,\" as the Quaker preacher said, \"of all women,\" to a voice in the government. And here, in this very first paragraph of the Declaration, is the assertion of the natural right of all to the ballot; for, how can \"the consent of the governed\" be given, if the right to vote be denied.\n\n[…]\n\nIt was we, the people, not we, the white male citizens, nor yet we, the male citizens, but we, the whole people, who formed this Union. And we formed it, not to give the blessings of liberty, but to secure them; not to the half of ourselves and the half of our posterity, but to the whole people—women as well as men. And it is downright mockery to talk to women of their enjoyment of the blessings of liberty while they are denied the use of the only means of securing them provided by this democratic republican government—the ballot.\n\n[…]\n\nFor any State to make sex a qualification that must ever result in the disfranchisement of one entire half of the people, is to pass a bill of attainder, or an ex post facto law, and is therefore a violation of the supreme law of the land. By it, the blessings of liberty are forever withheld from women and their female posterity. To them, this government has no just powers derived from the consent of the governed. To them this government is not a democracy. It is not a republic. It is an odious aristocracy; a hateful oligarchy; the most hateful ever established on the face of the globe. An oligarchy of wealth, where the rich govern the poor; an oligarchy of learning, where the educated govern the ignorant; or even an oligarchy of race, where the Saxon rules the African, might be endured; but surely this oligarchy of sex, which makes the men of every household sovereigns, masters; the women subjects, slaves; carrying dissension, rebellion into every home of the Nation, can not be endured. […]","caption":"Susan B. Anthony voted in Rochester, New York, in November 1872 and was arrested weeks later. Before her trial in June 1873, she gave this speech in dozens of towns in Monroe and Ontario counties.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"What is the central argument of Anthony's speech?","options":["Women already hold the right to vote as citizens.","Women should wait for each state to grant the vote.","Women should vote only in local town elections."],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that rights exist before any government?","options":["We throw to the winds the old dogma that governments can give rights.","They agree to abandon the methods of brute force in the adjustment of their differences, and adopt those of civilization.","To them this government is not a democracy."],"answer":0,"evidence":"We throw to the winds the old dogma that governments can give rights."},{"type":"author","q":"Why does Anthony quote the Declaration of Independence?","options":["To show that its authors meant to exclude women.","To show that founding words promise everyone a voice.","To show that the Declaration should be rewritten."],"answer":1},{"type":"craft","q":"What is the effect of the sentence that begins \"It was we, the people\"?","options":["It narrows the meaning of the Constitution to men.","It rejects narrow readings to insist on the whole people.","It lists the groups who first signed the Constitution."],"answer":1},{"type":"vocab","q":"What does \"disfranchisement\" most nearly mean in the speech?","options":["the loss of the right to vote","the loss of a business license","the loss of a trial by jury"],"answer":0,"evidence":"For any State to make sex a qualification that must ever result in the disfranchisement of one entire half of the people, is to pass a bill of attainder, or an ex post facto law, and is therefore a violation of the supreme law of the land."}],"grade":9,"words":652,"auto":{"fk":12.6,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g9-u3-c02","unit":"G9-U3","pool":"check","title":"The Four Freedoms","genre":"document","source":"Franklin D. Roosevelt, Annual Message to Congress on the State of the Union (\"Four Freedoms\"), January 6, 1941; text from the American Presidency Project, UC Santa Barbara; excerpt, gaps marked […]","license":"public-domain","text":"As men do not live by bread alone, they do not fight by armaments alone. Those who man our defenses, and those behind them who build our defenses, must have the stamina and the courage which come from unshakable belief in the manner of life which they are defending. The mighty action that we are calling for cannot be based on a disregard of all things worth fighting for.\n\nThe Nation takes great satisfaction and much strength from the things which have been done to make its people conscious of their individual stake in the preservation of democratic life in America. Those things have toughened the fibre of our people, have renewed their faith and strengthened their devotion to the institutions we make ready to protect.\n\nCertainly this is no time for any of us to stop thinking about the social and economic problems which are the root cause of the social revolution which is today a supreme factor in the world.\n\nFor there is nothing mysterious about the foundations of a healthy and strong democracy. The basic things expected by our people of their political and economic systems are simple. They are:\n\nEquality of opportunity for youth and for others.\nJobs for those who can work.\nSecurity for those who need it.\nThe ending of special privilege for the few.\nThe preservation of civil liberties for all.\n\nThe enjoyment of the fruits of scientific progress in a wider and constantly rising standard of living.\n\nThese are the simple, basic things that must never be lost sight of in the turmoil and unbelievable complexity of our modern world. The inner and abiding strength of our economic and political systems is dependent upon the degree to which they fulfill these expectations.\n\n[…]\n\nIn the future days, which we seek to make secure, we look forward to a world founded upon four essential human freedoms.\n\nThe first is freedom of speech and expression—everywhere in the world.\n\nThe second is freedom of every person to worship God in his own way—everywhere in the world.\n\nThe third is freedom from want—which, translated into world terms, means economic understandings which will secure to every nation a healthy peacetime life for its inhabitants—everywhere in the world.\n\nThe fourth is freedom from fear—which, translated into world terms, means a world-wide reduction of armaments to such a point and in such a thorough fashion that no nation will be in a position to commit an act of physical aggression against any neighbor—anywhere in the world.\n\nThat is no vision of a distant millennium. It is a definite basis for a kind of world attainable in our own time and generation. That kind of world is the very antithesis of the so-called new order of tyranny which the dictators seek to create with the crash of a bomb.\n\nTo that new order we oppose the greater conception—the moral order. A good society is able to face schemes of world domination and foreign revolutions alike without fear.\n\nSince the beginning of our American history, we have been engaged in change—in a perpetual peaceful revolution—a revolution which goes on steadily, quietly adjusting itself to changing conditions—without the concentration camp or the quick-lime in the ditch. The world order which we seek is the cooperation of free countries, working together in a friendly, civilized society.\n\nThis nation has placed its destiny in the hands and heads and hearts of its millions of free men and women; and its faith in freedom under the guidance of God. Freedom means the supremacy of human rights everywhere. Our support goes to those who struggle to gain those rights or keep them. Our strength is our unity of purpose. To that high concept there can be no end save victory.","caption":"President Franklin D. Roosevelt gave this State of the Union message to Congress on January 6, 1941, eleven months before the United States entered World War II.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"What central idea links the two parts of this excerpt?","options":["Freedom abroad matters more than problems at home.","A healthy democracy at home supports freedom worldwide.","Economic problems should wait until the war is over."],"answer":1},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that Roosevelt believed his goals could be reached soon?","options":["This nation has placed its destiny in the hands and heads and hearts of its millions of free men and women; and its faith in freedom under the guidance of God.","It is a definite basis for a kind of world attainable in our own time and generation.","To that new order we oppose the greater conception—the moral order."],"answer":1,"evidence":"It is a definite basis for a kind of world attainable in our own time and generation."},{"type":"craft","q":"What is the effect of repeating \"everywhere in the world\"?","options":["It stresses that the freedoms belong to all people.","It shows that America plans to rule other nations.","It suggests the freedoms are already won abroad."],"answer":0},{"type":"vocab","q":"What does \"antithesis\" most nearly mean in the speech?","options":["an early version","an exact opposite","a close partner"],"answer":1,"evidence":"That kind of world is the very antithesis of the so-called new order of tyranny which the dictators seek to create with the crash of a bomb."},{"type":"author","q":"Why does Roosevelt list \"the basic things expected by our people\" before naming the freedoms?","options":["To root the global freedoms in everyday needs at home.","To argue that Americans expect too much from government.","To explain why Congress should cut military spending."],"answer":0}],"grade":9,"words":636,"auto":{"fk":9.8,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g9-u3-c03","unit":"G9-U3","pool":"check","title":"How the Dust Bowl Taught Conservation","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"On the afternoon of April 14, 1935, a wall of dust more than a thousand feet high rolled across the southern Great Plains. Witnesses in Kansas, Oklahoma and Texas described the sky turning black in minutes. Birds fled ahead of the cloud, and people caught outdoors could not see their own hands. The day became known as Black Sunday, and it was only the worst of hundreds of dust storms that struck the region during the 1930s. Yet the disaster that came to be called the Dust Bowl was not caused by weather alone. It was the result of how people had treated the land, and the response to it changed the way Americans think about soil.\n\nFor thousands of years, the southern plains had been covered by grasses whose deep, tangled roots held the soil in place, even during droughts. In the early twentieth century, that changed. New tractors made it possible to plow enormous areas quickly, and high wheat prices during and after World War I encouraged farmers to tear up millions of acres of native grassland. For a while, the strategy worked. Rainfall was fairly good in the 1920s, and harvests were large. But when severe drought arrived in the early 1930s, the crops died, and nothing remained to anchor the dry, loose soil. Strong winds lifted it into the air and carried it hundreds of miles.\n\nThe human cost was enormous. Dust drifted against houses like snow, buried fences and filled kitchens even when windows were sealed with wet cloths. Many people developed lung illnesses that doctors called \"dust pneumonia.\" Farms failed by the thousands, many local banks that had lent money to farmers collapsed, and hundreds of thousands of people eventually left the region, many of them heading west toward California in search of work.\n\nOne man had been warning about this danger for years. Hugh Hammond Bennett, a soil scientist with the federal government, argued that soil erosion was a national threat, not just a local problem. According to an often-repeated story, Bennett was testifying before Congress in the spring of 1935 when a great dust cloud from the plains darkened the sky over Washington, D.C., giving lawmakers a vivid demonstration of his point. Whether the timing was really so perfect is uncertain, but Congress acted. On April 27, 1935, it passed the Soil Conservation Act, creating the Soil Conservation Service, with Bennett as its leader.\n\nThe new agency taught and paid farmers to use methods that protected the soil. Contour plowing, in which furrows follow the natural curves of the land instead of running in straight lines, slowed the flow of water and kept soil from washing away. Farmers rotated crops and planted cover crops to keep the ground protected between harvests. Strips of grass were left between fields to catch blowing dirt. In a separate federal effort, workers planted more than two hundred million trees in long rows called shelterbelts, stretching across the plains from Canada to Texas, to break the force of the wind.\n\nPerhaps the most lasting change was the idea that farmers could not protect the land alone. Beginning in 1937, communities formed local soil conservation districts, in which neighbors agreed to follow shared practices, because erosion on one farm could bury the next. Thousands of these districts still exist today.\n\nBy the end of the decade, the rains returned and the worst storms ended. The Dust Bowl left a painful lesson. Land that seems endlessly productive can be ruined within a few years if its natural defenses are destroyed. It also left a hopeful one: careful science and cooperation can repair much of the damage. The agency Bennett led still exists, now called the Natural Resources Conservation Service, and the practices it promoted remain part of farming across the country.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"What is the central idea of the passage?","options":["The Dust Bowl was caused only by an unusually long drought.","Human choices helped cause the Dust Bowl, and it led to conservation.","Most families on the plains were unaffected by the dust storms."],"answer":1},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that people helped cause the disaster?","options":["Farms failed by the thousands, many local banks that had lent money to farmers collapsed, and hundreds of thousands of people eventually left the region, many of them heading west toward California in search of work.","New tractors made it possible to plow enormous areas quickly, and high wheat prices during and after World War I encouraged farmers to tear up millions of acres of native grassland.","Strong winds lifted it into the air and carried it hundreds of miles."],"answer":1,"evidence":"New tractors made it possible to plow enormous areas quickly, and high wheat prices during and after World War I encouraged farmers to tear up millions of acres of native grassland."},{"type":"author","q":"Why does the author call the timing of the Bennett story uncertain?","options":["to signal that a popular story may be embellished","to argue that Congress ignored the dust storms","to suggest that Bennett invented the dust storms"],"answer":0},{"type":"inference","q":"Why were local soil conservation districts important?","options":["They let each farmer avoid following any rules.","They moved families from the plains to California.","They reflected that one farm's erosion affects others."],"answer":2},{"type":"vocab","q":"In paragraph 2, what does the word \"anchor\" most nearly mean?","options":["hold in place","carry far away","soak deeply"],"answer":0,"evidence":"But when severe drought arrived in the early 1930s, the crops died, and nothing remained to anchor the dry, loose soil."}],"grade":9,"words":633,"auto":{"fk":7.8,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g9-u5-p01","unit":"G9-U5","pool":"practice","title":"Closing the Stand","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"The last weekend of September, the beach empties the way a theater does after the final scene: slowly at first, then all at once, until only the people who work there are left sweeping up what everyone else dropped. Inés has worked at her grandmother's snack stand every summer since she was eleven. This year, at fifteen, she is trusted with the keys.\n\nThe stand is called La Ola, The Wave, and it is not much to look at. It is a plywood box painted the green of old mint ice cream, with a hinged window that props open on a broom handle and a hand-lettered menu that has not changed in twenty years. The menu offers mango with chili, buttered corn on the cob, and lemonade so sour it makes children's faces fold in on themselves like paper fans. On a good July afternoon, the line stretches past the lifeguard tower. Today, there is no line at all.\n\nHer grandmother, Abuela Rosa, sits on an overturned crate counting the cash box, licking her thumb before each bill. She does not hurry; she has never hurried in her life, Inés thinks, not even the year the hurricane tore away the roof and scattered its shingles across three parking lots. Rosa says that hurrying is just a way of arriving tired.\n\n\"Start with the freezer,\" Rosa says without looking up.\n\nThe freezer is the hardest job, and both of them know it. It hums in the back corner, a white chest as old as Inés's mother, its lid scarred with stickers from every summer the stand has survived. Inés lifts the lid and a breath of cold rises against her face, smelling of frost and vanilla and the faint metallic tang of old ice. She chips the frost from the walls with a wooden spoon. Each piece falls with a small, dry click, like a clock that has decided to keep time by dropping its seconds one at a time.\n\nOut on the water, a single surfer waits for a wave that does not come. The ocean is the flat gray of a sidewalk. Gulls stand in the wet sand with their shoulders hunched, looking like old men waiting for a bus that stopped running years ago.\n\nInés works steadily through the afternoon, stacking the plastic chairs in towers and wiping the counter until the wood reveals its grain. She takes down the menu board and finds, beneath it, the pencil marks where her mother measured her height against the wall each summer: a ladder of short lines and dates that climbs from her knee to her shoulder and then simply stops at the year she turned twelve, the year her mother moved away for work. Inés runs her finger up the ladder, but she does not add a new line.\n\nWhen the sun drops behind the boardwalk, the light turns the color of weak tea. Rosa finally closes the cash box and stands, one hand pressed to the small of her back.\n\n\"Every year,\" Rosa says, \"people ask me if it is sad, closing up.\"\n\n\"Is it?\"\n\nRosa considers the question the way she considers a mango at the market, turning it over, pressing it gently with her thumb. \"Closing is not the sad part,\" she says at last. \"The sad part would be if there were nothing worth closing.\"\n\nTogether they lower the window. The broom handle comes down, and the hinged panel swings shut with a sound like a book being closed on a story that both of them already know by heart. Rosa snaps the padlock, and Inés writes the date in marker on the inside of the door, beneath a column of other dates in other handwriting, some faded nearly to nothing.\n\nThey walk up the beach toward the parking lot, their shadows stretching long and thin ahead of them, reaching for the road. Behind them, the stand grows smaller, a green square against the dimming water, holding the summer inside it like a held breath.\n\nInés does not look back, because she does not need to; she knows exactly where it will be waiting in June.","questions":[{"type":"craft","q":"What does comparing the empty beach to a theater after the final scene suggest?","options":["The beach is being set up for an outdoor play.","The summer season has ended like a finished show.","The workers at the beach are trained as actors."],"answer":1},{"type":"craft","q":"What is the effect of comparing the falling frost to a clock dropping its seconds?","options":["It shows that the freezer has a broken timer.","It suggests that Inés is late for an appointment.","It links the chore to the passing of time."],"answer":2},{"type":"inference","q":"Why does Inés leave the height chart without a new line?","options":["The marks are tied to her absent mother.","Rosa forbids anyone to write on the wall.","She has grown too tall to fit on the wall."],"answer":0},{"type":"theme","q":"Which theme does Rosa's comment about closing best express?","options":["An ending shows how much something was valued.","Hard work matters more than time with family.","It is wiser to leave a place than to return."],"answer":0}],"grade":9,"words":692,"auto":{"fk":7,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g9-u5-p02","unit":"G9-U5","pool":"practice","title":"Whitman's Working City","genre":"poetry","source":"Walt Whitman, \"I Hear America Singing,\" \"Sparkles from the Wheel,\" \"To a Locomotive in Winter\" and \"Mannahatta\" (\"I was asking for something specific\"), from Leaves of Grass (1891–92 edition), Project Gutenberg eBook #1322","license":"public-domain","text":"## I Hear America Singing\n\nI hear America singing, the varied carols I hear,\nThose of mechanics, each one singing his as it should be blithe and strong,\nThe carpenter singing his as he measures his plank or beam,\nThe mason singing his as he makes ready for work, or leaves off work,\nThe boatman singing what belongs to him in his boat, the deckhand singing on the steamboat deck,\nThe shoemaker singing as he sits on his bench, the hatter singing as he stands,\nThe wood-cutter's song, the ploughboy's on his way in the morning, or at noon intermission or at sundown,\nThe delicious singing of the mother, or of the young wife at work, or of the girl sewing or washing,\nEach singing what belongs to him or her and to none else,\nThe day what belongs to the day—at night the party of young fellows, robust, friendly,\nSinging with open mouths their strong melodious songs.\n\n## Sparkles from the Wheel\n\nWhere the city's ceaseless crowd moves on the livelong day,\nWithdrawn I join a group of children watching, I pause aside with them.\n\nBy the curb toward the edge of the flagging,\nA knife-grinder works at his wheel sharpening a great knife,\nBending over he carefully holds it to the stone, by foot and knee,\nWith measur'd tread he turns rapidly, as he presses with light but firm hand,\nForth issue then in copious golden jets,\nSparkles from the wheel.\n\nThe scene and all its belongings, how they seize and affect me,\nThe sad sharp-chinn'd old man with worn clothes and broad shoulder-band of leather,\nMyself effusing and fluid, a phantom curiously floating, now here absorb'd and arrested,\nThe group, (an unminded point set in a vast surrounding,)\nThe attentive, quiet children, the loud, proud, restive base of the streets,\nThe low hoarse purr of the whirling stone, the light-press'd blade,\nDiffusing, dropping, sideways-darting, in tiny showers of gold,\nSparkles from the wheel.\n\n## To a Locomotive in Winter\n\nThee for my recitative,\nThee in the driving storm even as now, the snow, the winter-day declining,\nThee in thy panoply, thy measur'd dual throbbing and thy beat convulsive,\nThy black cylindric body, golden brass and silvery steel,\nThy ponderous side-bars, parallel and connecting rods, gyrating, shuttling at thy sides,\nThy metrical, now swelling pant and roar, now tapering in the distance,\nThy great protruding head-light fix'd in front,\nThy long, pale, floating vapor-pennants, tinged with delicate purple,\nThe dense and murky clouds out-belching from thy smoke-stack,\nThy knitted frame, thy springs and valves, the tremulous twinkle of thy wheels,\nThy train of cars behind, obedient, merrily following,\nThrough gale or calm, now swift, now slack, yet steadily careering;\nType of the modern—emblem of motion and power—pulse of the continent,\nFor once come serve the Muse and merge in verse, even as here I see thee,\nWith storm and buffeting gusts of wind and falling snow,\nBy day thy warning ringing bell to sound its notes,\nBy night thy silent signal lamps to swing.\n\nFierce-throated beauty!\nRoll through my chant with all thy lawless music, thy swinging lamps at night,\nThy madly-whistled laughter, echoing, rumbling like an earthquake, rousing all,\nLaw of thyself complete, thine own track firmly holding,\n(No sweetness debonair of tearful harp or glib piano thine,)\nThy trills of shrieks by rocks and hills return'd,\nLaunch'd o'er the prairies wide, across the lakes,\nTo the free skies unpent and glad and strong.\n\n## Mannahatta\n\nI was asking for something specific and perfect for my city,\nWhereupon lo! upsprang the aboriginal name.\n\nNow I see what there is in a name, a word, liquid, sane, unruly, musical, self-sufficient,\nI see that the word of my city is that word from of old,\nBecause I see that word nested in nests of water-bays, superb,\nRich, hemm'd thick all around with sailships and steamships, an island sixteen miles long, solid-founded,\nNumberless crowded streets, high growths of iron, slender, strong, light, splendidly uprising toward clear skies,\nTides swift and ample, well-loved by me, toward sundown,\nThe flowing sea-currents, the little islands, larger adjoining islands, the heights, the villas,\nThe countless masts, the white shore-steamers, the lighters, the ferry-boats, the black sea-steamers well-model'd,\nThe down-town streets, the jobbers' houses of business, the houses of business of the ship-merchants and money-brokers, the river-streets,\nImmigrants arriving, fifteen or twenty thousand in a week,\nThe carts hauling goods, the manly race of drivers of horses, the brown-faced sailors,\nThe summer air, the bright sun shining, and the sailing clouds aloft,\nThe winter snows, the sleigh-bells, the broken ice in the river, passing along up or down with the flood-tide or ebb-tide,\nThe mechanics of the city, the masters, well-form'd, beautiful-faced, looking you straight in the eyes,\nTrottoirs throng'd, vehicles, Broadway, the women, the shops and shows,\nA million people—manners free and superb—open voices—hospitality— the most courageous and friendly young men,\nCity of hurried and sparkling waters! city of spires and masts!\nCity nested in bays! my city!","caption":"Walt Whitman (1819–1892) revised and expanded Leaves of Grass for most of his life. These four poems come from its final edition.","questions":[{"type":"theme","q":"What do these four poems most clearly share?","options":["They celebrate work, machines and city life.","They mourn the loss of quiet farm villages.","They warn readers about the dangers of trains."],"answer":0},{"type":"craft","q":"Why does Whitman repeat \"singing\" throughout \"I Hear America Singing\"?","options":["To show that the workers dislike their jobs.","To present each worker's labor as a personal song.","To describe a choir that performs on the steamboat."],"answer":1},{"type":"craft","q":"What is the effect of addressing the locomotive as \"thee\" and \"Fierce-throated beauty\"?","options":["It treats the machine as a worthy subject for a poem.","It shows that the speaker is afraid to ride the train.","It suggests that the train is old and out of date."],"answer":0},{"type":"inference","q":"In \"Sparkles from the Wheel,\" how does the speaker feel while watching?","options":["bored and eager to move on","absorbed, as if outside himself","angry at the noisy old grinder"],"answer":1}],"grade":9,"words":869,"auto":{"fk":27,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g9-u5-c01","unit":"G9-U5","pool":"check","title":"Whitman on Wonder and the Self","genre":"poetry","source":"Walt Whitman, \"Song of Myself\" (sections 1 and 52), \"When I Heard the Learn'd Astronomer,\" \"Miracles\" and \"A Noiseless Patient Spider,\" from Leaves of Grass (1891–92 edition), Project Gutenberg eBook #1322; sections 1 and 52 are excerpts","license":"public-domain","text":"## From \"Song of Myself,\" section 1\n\nI celebrate myself, and sing myself,\nAnd what I assume you shall assume,\nFor every atom belonging to me as good belongs to you.\n\nI loafe and invite my soul,\nI lean and loafe at my ease observing a spear of summer grass.\n\nMy tongue, every atom of my blood, form'd from this soil, this air,\nBorn here of parents born here from parents the same, and their parents the same,\nI, now thirty-seven years old in perfect health begin,\nHoping to cease not till death.\n\nCreeds and schools in abeyance,\nRetiring back a while sufficed at what they are, but never forgotten,\nI harbor for good or bad, I permit to speak at every hazard,\nNature without check with original energy.\n\n## From \"Song of Myself,\" section 52\n\nThe spotted hawk swoops by and accuses me, he complains of my gab and my loitering.\n\nI too am not a bit tamed, I too am untranslatable,\nI sound my barbaric yawp over the roofs of the world.\n\nThe last scud of day holds back for me,\nIt flings my likeness after the rest and true as any on the shadow'd wilds,\nIt coaxes me to the vapor and the dusk.\n\nI depart as air, I shake my white locks at the runaway sun,\nI effuse my flesh in eddies, and drift it in lacy jags.\n\nI bequeath myself to the dirt to grow from the grass I love,\nIf you want me again look for me under your boot-soles.\n\nYou will hardly know who I am or what I mean,\nBut I shall be good health to you nevertheless,\nAnd filter and fibre your blood.\n\nFailing to fetch me at first keep encouraged,\nMissing me one place search another,\nI stop somewhere waiting for you.\n\n## When I Heard the Learn'd Astronomer\n\nWhen I heard the learn'd astronomer,\nWhen the proofs, the figures, were ranged in columns before me,\nWhen I was shown the charts and diagrams, to add, divide, and measure them,\nWhen I sitting heard the astronomer where he lectured with much applause in the lecture-room,\nHow soon unaccountable I became tired and sick,\nTill rising and gliding out I wander'd off by myself,\nIn the mystical moist night-air, and from time to time,\nLook'd up in perfect silence at the stars.\n\n## Miracles\n\nWhy, who makes much of a miracle?\nAs to me I know of nothing else but miracles,\nWhether I walk the streets of Manhattan,\nOr dart my sight over the roofs of houses toward the sky,\nOr wade with naked feet along the beach just in the edge of the water,\nOr stand under trees in the woods,\nOr talk by day with any one I love, or sleep in the bed at night with any one I love,\nOr sit at table at dinner with the rest,\nOr look at strangers opposite me riding in the car,\nOr watch honey-bees busy around the hive of a summer forenoon,\nOr animals feeding in the fields,\nOr birds, or the wonderfulness of insects in the air,\nOr the wonderfulness of the sundown, or of stars shining so quiet and bright,\nOr the exquisite delicate thin curve of the new moon in spring;\nThese with the rest, one and all, are to me miracles,\nThe whole referring, yet each distinct and in its place.\n\nTo me every hour of the light and dark is a miracle,\nEvery cubic inch of space is a miracle,\nEvery square yard of the surface of the earth is spread with the same,\nEvery foot of the interior swarms with the same.\nTo me the sea is a continual miracle,\nThe fishes that swim—the rocks—the motion of the waves—the ships with men in them,\nWhat stranger miracles are there?\n\n## A Noiseless Patient Spider\n\nA noiseless patient spider,\nI mark'd where on a little promontory it stood isolated,\nMark'd how to explore the vacant vast surrounding,\nIt launch'd forth filament, filament, filament out of itself,\nEver unreeling them, ever tirelessly speeding them.\n\nAnd you O my soul where you stand,\nSurrounded, detached, in measureless oceans of space,\nCeaselessly musing, venturing, throwing, seeking the spheres to connect them,\nTill the bridge you will need be form'd, till the ductile anchor hold,\nTill the gossamer thread you fling catch somewhere, O my soul.","caption":"\"Song of Myself\" is a long poem in 52 numbered sections. Only the first and last sections appear here.","questions":[{"type":"theme","q":"Which idea runs through all of these poems?","options":["Wonder comes from direct experience of the world.","Science explains everything worth knowing about stars.","A person should stay apart from other people."],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that the speaker expects to live on in nature?","options":["I too am not a bit tamed, I too am untranslatable, I sound my barbaric yawp over the roofs of the world.","I depart as air, I shake my white locks at the runaway sun, I effuse my flesh in eddies, and drift it in lacy jags.","I bequeath myself to the dirt to grow from the grass I love, If you want me again look for me under your boot-soles."],"answer":2,"evidence":"I bequeath myself to the dirt to grow from the grass I love, If you want me again look for me under your boot-soles."},{"type":"craft","q":"How does the structure of \"When I Heard the Learn'd Astronomer\" shape its meaning?","options":["Rhymed pairs of lines make the lecture sound joyful.","Repeated \"When\" lines build tension the calm ending releases.","Short questions show the speaker testing the astronomer."],"answer":1},{"type":"compare","q":"In \"A Noiseless Patient Spider,\" how is the soul like the spider?","options":["Both stay hidden so that no one can find them.","Both build traps to catch whatever passes by.","Both send out threads, seeking a place to connect."],"answer":2},{"type":"vocab","q":"In section 52, what does \"yawp\" most nearly mean?","options":["a loud, wild cry","a quiet, sad song","a long, slow bow"],"answer":0,"evidence":"I too am not a bit tamed, I too am untranslatable, I sound my barbaric yawp over the roofs of the world."}],"grade":9,"words":730,"auto":{"fk":16.1,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g9-u5-c02","unit":"G9-U5","pool":"check","title":"Copies Made While You Wait","genre":"poetry","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"## I. The Counter\n\nAll summer I stood behind the key machine\nat Delgado Hardware, under a sign\nthat promised COPIES MADE WHILE YOU WAIT,\nas if waiting were the easy part.\n\nPeople handed me their keys the way\nyou hand a stranger a photograph:\nquickly, then watching to see\nwhat you will do with it.\n\n## II. How It Works\n\nMr. Delgado taught me on the first day.\nClamp the old key on the left, the blank on the right.\nLine up the shoulders. Tighten. Breathe.\nThen let the guide trace every valley of the old key\nwhile the cutting wheel, a small loud sun,\ncarves the same valleys into the new one.\n\nThe old key teaches. The blank learns.\nNothing is invented here.\nThat is the whole point, he said.\nA key that is creative is a key that does not work.\n\nHe brushed the brass dust from the tray\ninto his palm, gold as pollen,\nand dropped it in a coffee can\nhe had been filling for thirty-one years.\n\n## III. Customers\n\nA woman in scrubs, still wearing her badge,\nneeded four copies of one key:\none for each of her grown children,\nbecause her mother could not be left alone anymore.\nShe did not say this. The four keys said it.\n\nA man asked me to copy a key\nthat no longer opened anything.\nHe had kept it on his ring for nineteen years.\nHe wanted a spare, he said, in case he lost it.\nI did not ask what door it had belonged to.\nI cut it slowly, as if the door were listening.\n\nTwo roommates, laughing, argued\nover whose key should be the master.\nI told them the copy is exactly the same.\nThey did not believe me. They are probably right.\n\nA boy about my age bought one blank\nand watched me cut it, not saying anything,\nand when I handed it to him,\nwarm from the wheel, he held it\nthe way you hold a ticket\nto somewhere you are not sure you should go.\n\n## IV. What the Machine Cannot Copy\n\nThe machine copies the cuts, the depth, the spacing.\nIt does not copy the scratches,\nthe little bend from the night somebody\nforced it in a frozen lock,\nthe shine on the bow where a thumb\npressed it every morning for years.\n\nMr. Delgado says the old locksmiths\ncould read a key the way you read a face,\ncould tell you what kind of house it came from,\nhow long the family had lived there,\nwhether anyone had ever changed the locks.\nI can only read the number stamped on the blank.\nI am still learning to see the rest.\n\nEvery copy leaves the store brand new,\ncold, without a single story.\nIt will open the same door.\nIt will not remember anything about it.\n\nI thought about that while I swept the floor at closing.\nThe original key goes home with its owner.\nThe copy goes out into the world to begin.\n\n## V. Last Day\n\nOn my last day, Mr. Delgado\nhanded me a blank and said, Cut whatever you want.\nI took my own house key off my ring,\nthe one my father had copied for me\nwhen I was eleven, at this same counter,\nfrom his.\n\nSo I made a copy of a copy of a copy.\nThe wheel sang the old song.\nThe valleys came out the same,\nthe way a family's habits come out the same,\nthe way a voice comes out of a child\nand sounds, for a second, like a parent's.\n\nI do not know yet what door it is for.\nBut it fits my hand.\nAnd it is warm.","caption":"A poem sequence in five parts, spoken by a teenager who spends a summer cutting keys at a neighborhood hardware store.","questions":[{"type":"theme","q":"Which theme does the sequence develop most fully?","options":["Copies can repeat a form but carry no history.","Hard work in a store matters more than school.","Machines will soon replace all skilled workers."],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best shows the speaker reading a customer's life from her request?","options":["She did not say this.","The four keys said it.","They did not believe me."],"answer":1,"evidence":"The four keys said it."},{"type":"craft","q":"What is the effect of calling the cutting wheel \"a small loud sun\"?","options":["It hints that the store is too hot in summer.","It makes the machine seem bright, fierce and central.","It shows that the speaker is afraid of the wheel."],"answer":1},{"type":"craft","q":"How does the structure of Part V build on Part IV?","options":["It drops the key image to describe a new job.","It repeats Part IV's lines word for word.","It answers Part IV with a copy that carries meaning."],"answer":2},{"type":"inference","q":"Why does the speaker cut the key for the man \"slowly, as if the door were listening\"?","options":["The speaker senses the key holds deep personal meaning.","The speaker is still learning how the machine works.","The speaker suspects the man plans to break into a home."],"answer":0}],"grade":9,"words":614,"auto":{"fk":3.4,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g9-u5-c03","unit":"G9-U5","pool":"check","title":"The Interpreter","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"The waiting room at the county clinic smells like hand sanitizer and burnt coffee. Mariam has been here for an hour and forty minutes, long enough to memorize the poster on the opposite wall, a cartoon heart giving a thumbs-up beneath the words YOUR HEALTH IS IN YOUR HANDS. Beside her, her mother sits very straight, her purse held on her lap with both hands, as if someone might try to take it.\n\nMariam is fifteen, and she has been translating for her mother since she was eight. She translated at the bank, at parent-teacher conferences where she was the subject of the conversation, at the hardware store when the kitchen faucet broke, and at the landlord's office when the heat stopped working in February. She has become, without ever applying for the job, the bridge between her mother and the English-speaking world. Some days the bridge feels sturdy; other days it feels like a rope stretched across a canyon, swaying under a weight it was never designed to carry.\n\n\"Rahimi?\" a nurse calls, mispronouncing the name so that it sounds like a question. Mariam's mother rises before Mariam can stand, and the two of them follow the nurse down a hallway lined with closed doors.\n\nThe doctor is young and tired, with kind eyes and a voice that moves too quickly. She talks about test results and blood pressure and something called a beta blocker. Mariam listens, holding each sentence in her head the way you might carry a full glass of water across a crowded room, careful not to spill a single word. Then she turns to her mother and pours the sentences out again in Dari.\n\nBut translation is never just pouring. Some words have no matching container. The doctor says \"manageable,\" and Mariam searches for an equivalent that will not sound like \"minor\" or, worse, like \"serious.\" The doctor says \"lifestyle changes,\" and Mariam must decide whether to explain the phrase or simply list the changes: less salt, more walking, the small white pill each morning. Every choice is a small act of authorship. Mariam is not only repeating the doctor's words. She is deciding which version of the truth her mother will hear.\n\nHer mother asks a question, and Mariam hesitates. The question is whether the condition is what killed her mother's brother, Mariam's uncle, in Herat eleven years ago. Mariam has never heard her mother speak of this. She looks at the doctor, who is waiting, pen raised, and at her mother, whose face is perfectly still, the way a lake is still when the wind has not yet decided where to blow.\n\nMariam translates the question exactly. It is the hardest sentence she has ever carried.\n\nThe doctor's voice slows down as she explains that she cannot know about the uncle, but that high blood pressure is common and treatable, and that her mother has caught it early. Mariam translates this too, and she watches her mother's hands relax on the purse, one finger at a time, like a knot slowly coming loose.\n\nOn the bus home, her mother looks out the window at the gray city sliding by. After a long silence, she says something Mariam does not expect.\n\n\"You chose your words carefully today,\" her mother says in Dari. \"I could hear you choosing.\"\n\nMariam feels her face grow warm. She had always assumed the choosing was invisible, a private labor that took place somewhere between one language and the other. She had never imagined that her mother could hear the hesitations, the pauses, the small adjustments, the way a musician can hear a note being tuned.\n\n\"I didn't want to scare you,\" Mariam says.\n\nHer mother nods slowly. \"I know,\" she says. \"But next time, give me the hard words too. I can carry them. You are not the only strong one in this family.\"\n\nMariam looks down at her own hands. All these years she has thought of herself as the bridge. It has never occurred to her that a bridge is held up from both sides.\n\nThe bus turns onto their street. Through the window, the afternoon light catches on the river, and for a moment the water is bright and wide and easy to cross.","questions":[{"type":"craft","q":"What does the metaphor of Mariam as a \"bridge\" suggest?","options":["She hopes to become an engineer someday.","She links her mother to the English-speaking world.","She prefers to live between two neighborhoods."],"answer":1},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that translating involves making choices?","options":["The doctor is young and tired, with kind eyes and a voice that moves too quickly.","She is deciding which version of the truth her mother will hear.","On the bus home, her mother looks out the window at the gray city sliding by."],"answer":1,"evidence":"She is deciding which version of the truth her mother will hear."},{"type":"craft","q":"What does the simile of a knot \"slowly coming loose\" show?","options":["Her mother is untying the strap of her purse.","Her mother is growing angry with the doctor.","Her mother's fear is gradually easing."],"answer":2},{"type":"theme","q":"Which theme does the ending of the story develop?","options":["Strength in a family can flow in both directions.","Children should stop translating for their parents.","Doctors must speak more slowly to every patient."],"answer":0},{"type":"inference","q":"What does the mother's remark \"I could hear you choosing\" reveal?","options":["She understood every English word the doctor said.","She noticed Mariam softening some of the news.","She was annoyed that Mariam spoke too slowly."],"answer":1}],"grade":9,"words":711,"auto":{"fk":6.3,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g9-book-ch1","unit":"G9-U2","pool":"book","title":"Site 4","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","book":"The Weight of Small Numbers","chapter":1,"text":"Every Saturday morning for seven weeks, Nadia Haddad and Jonah Park had knelt on the muddy banks of Alder Creek with a plastic tub of test tubes, a thermometer and a notebook whose pages had gone soft and wavy from the damp. They had chosen the creek for their regional science fair project because it was close, because it was free, and because their biology teacher, Ms. Okafor, had said something in September that neither of them could stop thinking about: \"The most interesting questions are usually hiding in places you walk past every day.\"\n\nAlder Creek was one of those places. It began as a trickle in the hills north of town, slid past a row of old farms, curved behind the Westgate shopping plaza, and then wandered through Heron Marsh, a wetland the city had restored three years earlier by tearing out a parking lot and replanting native grasses. After the marsh, the creek widened and slowed before emptying into the river.\n\nTheir question was simple. Did the restored marsh actually clean the water? To find out, they tested five sites along the creek, from the farms at the top to the river at the bottom. At each site they measured temperature, dissolved oxygen and nitrate levels, carefully recording every number in the notebook and later typing it into a shared spreadsheet. Nitrates, Ms. Okafor had explained, often wash into streams from fertilizer and sewage. In large amounts they feed explosive growth of algae, which can choke a waterway of oxygen.\n\nFor six weeks, the numbers had told a satisfying story. Nitrate levels were highest near the farms, dropped a little behind the plaza, and fell sharply after the water passed through the marsh. Dissolved oxygen moved in the opposite direction, rising as the water flowed downstream. Jonah, who loved graphs the way some people love music, had already designed a chart in which the two lines crossed neatly in the middle, like a pair of scissors.\n\n\"Look at that,\" he had said, turning his laptop toward her in the library. \"That's a winning chart. Judges see that and they understand the whole project in five seconds.\"\n\nNadia had to admit it was beautiful. It was also, she thought, exactly what they had hoped to find, and that fact made her slightly uneasy, though she could not have explained why.\n\nOn the seventh Saturday, it had rained hard overnight, and the creek was running high and brown. They worked quickly, their sneakers soaked, the water louder than usual. At Site 4, just downstream from the culvert that carried the creek under the plaza's back lot, Nadia filled a test tube, added the reagent and watched the liquid turn a shade of pink so deep that she held it up twice against the color chart to make sure.\n\n\"Jonah,\" she called, \"come and look at this.\"\n\nHe crouched beside her and frowned. According to the chart, the nitrate level at Site 4 was nearly four times higher than it had been the week before, higher even than the reading at the farms.\n\n\"That can't be right,\" Jonah said. \"Maybe the tube was dirty, or maybe the reagent is too old.\"\n\n\"We used the same reagent at Site 3 ten minutes ago.\"\n\n\"Then maybe you added too much.\" He shrugged, already standing. \"It happens, and it's one number out of almost two hundred.\"\n\nNadia wrote the reading in the notebook anyway, pressing the pencil hard enough to dent the page. Then, because there was nothing else to do, she capped the tube, packed the tub and followed Jonah toward Site 5, where the marsh opened out on either side of them, green and quiet and full of red-winged blackbirds calling from the reeds.\n\nLater, walking home, she kept turning the number over in her mind the way she might turn a smooth stone in her pocket. Scientists made mistakes, she knew; tubes got contaminated and hands slipped. But a mistake, she thought, should be random, and something about the deep pink color in that tube had not felt random at all. It had felt like the creek was trying to tell them something, and she had no idea yet what it was.\n\nThat night she opened the spreadsheet and typed the reading into the cell for Site 4, Week 7. On Jonah's chart, the scissors no longer crossed so neatly. One line now jumped upward in a sharp, ugly spike, like a heartbeat on a hospital monitor.\n\nShe stared at the jagged line for a long time before she finally closed the laptop.","questions":[{"type":"why","q":"Why do Nadia and Jonah test five different sites along the creek?","options":["to find the deepest place to collect samples","to compare the water before and after the marsh","to count which site has the most blackbirds"],"answer":1},{"type":"inference","q":"Why does the neat scissors chart make Nadia uneasy?","options":["It matches their hopes a little too perfectly.","It uses colors that the judges may dislike.","It leaves out the readings from the farms."],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that Nadia doubts the reading was a simple mistake?","options":["They worked quickly, their sneakers soaked, the water louder than usual.","Then, because there was nothing else to do, she capped the tube, packed the tub and followed Jonah toward Site 5, where the marsh opened out on either side of them, green and quiet and full of red-winged blackbirds calling from the reeds.","But a mistake, she thought, should be random, and something about the deep pink color in that tube had not felt random at all."],"answer":2,"evidence":"But a mistake, she thought, should be random, and something about the deep pink color in that tube had not felt random at all."},{"type":"craft","q":"What does comparing the spike to \"a heartbeat on a hospital monitor\" suggest?","options":["Nadia is worried about her own health.","The odd reading feels alive and urgent.","The chart was printed at a hospital."],"answer":1}],"grade":9,"words":764,"auto":{"fk":7.2,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g9-book-ch2","unit":"G9-U2","pool":"book","title":"The Smoothed Line","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","book":"The Weight of Small Numbers","chapter":2,"text":"The science fair entries were due at midnight on Friday, and on Thursday evening Nadia and Jonah sat across from each other at the long wooden table in the public library, surrounded by poster board, glue sticks and the remains of a bag of pretzels. Their poster was nearly finished, and its title, \"Does Heron Marsh Clean Alder Creek,\" stretched across the top in letters Jonah had printed and trimmed with a precision that bordered on obsession.\n\nAll that remained was the central chart, which Jonah had volunteered to finalize while Nadia, absorbed in writing the conclusion, did not glance at his screen for nearly an hour. When she finally leaned over to check the graph, she stopped, because the scissors were back. The two lines crossed cleanly in the middle, exactly as they had before the seventh Saturday, and the spike at Site 4 had disappeared.\n\n\"Where's Week 7?\" she asked, and Jonah answered without looking up that it was there.\n\nShe pulled the laptop toward her and opened the spreadsheet. The cell for Site 4, Week 7 no longer held the number she had typed; instead it contained a figure that was almost exactly the average of Weeks 6 and 8, with no note, no comment and nothing to indicate that anything had ever been different.\n\n\"You replaced it,\" she said quietly.\n\n\"I smoothed it.\" Jonah finally met her eyes, and she could see that he had been expecting this conversation and dreading it. \"Nadia, it was an outlier, one bad reading, probably from a contaminated tube, and scientists throw out outliers all the time.\"\n\n\"They do, when they can demonstrate that it's an error, and they report that they did it,\" she said. \"You just erased it.\"\n\n\"Because it makes the whole project look like a mess!\" His voice rose, and a man reading a newspaper at the next table glanced over, so Jonah lowered it. \"Think about the judges, who have about four minutes per poster. If they see a giant spike, they'll start asking about contamination and procedures, and they'll stop noticing everything else we accomplished in seven weeks of work.\"\n\nShe understood the argument, which made it harder to answer. Part of her wanted to agree, to let the clean chart stand and go home and sleep, because the spike was ugly, it complicated their conclusion, and Jonah was right that they could not prove it was real. But they could not prove it was false, either.\n\n\"What if it's real?\" she asked. \"What if something actually happened at Site 4?\"\n\nHe tried to joke about a nitrate monster, but the smile came out crooked, and then his shoulders dropped and he rubbed his eyes with the heels of his hands. \"Look, the first-place winner gets a paid position in the summer research program at the university, with a real laboratory. My dad has been talking about it since August, and he even tells his coworkers about it. If I come home with an honorable mention because of one strange test tube, I honestly don't know what I'll say to him.\"\n\nNadia had not known about the program, or about his father. She looked at Jonah, at the careful letters he had cut for the title, at the graph he had constructed line by line, and she felt the problem shift in her hands. It was no longer only a question about a number; it was a question about a person she liked and respected, who was frightened, and who was asking her, without quite saying so, to share the weight of a decision he already felt guilty about.\n\n\"I need to think,\" she said, and when he protested that the project was due tomorrow at midnight, she promised that she would think fast.\n\nShe gathered her things and walked out into the cold evening, the library doors sighing shut behind her. Along the sidewalk, the storm drains were still gurgling from the afternoon's rain, and she realized that somewhere beneath the street, that water was flowing toward Alder Creek, past the plaza, past Site 4, carrying whatever it had collected along the way.\n\nShe stopped walking, suddenly remembering that Week 7 had come after a night of heavy rain. When she checked the weather app on her phone, it reported that more rain had fallen today than on any day since then.\n\nIf the spike had been caused by a contaminated tube, it would not happen again. But if it had been caused by the rain, then right now, in the darkness, the creek might be doing precisely the same thing.","questions":[{"type":"inference","q":"What does Jonah's choice of the word \"smoothed\" instead of \"replaced\" reveal?","options":["He believes the data were simply typed wrong.","He plans to tell the judges what he changed.","He wants his change to sound harmless."],"answer":2},{"type":"why","q":"Why does Jonah want the clean chart so badly?","options":["He fears letting his father down over the prize.","He thinks the spike came from Nadia's own error.","He wants to finish early and go home to sleep."],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best shows that the conflict has become personal for Nadia?","options":["The science fair entries were due at midnight on Friday, and on Thursday evening Nadia and Jonah sat across from each other at the long wooden table in the public library, surrounded by poster board, glue sticks and the remains of a bag of pretzels.","It was no longer only a question about a number; it was a question about a person she liked and respected, who was frightened, and who was asking her, without quite saying so, to share the weight of a decision he already felt guilty about.","Along the sidewalk, the storm drains were still gurgling from the afternoon's rain, and she realized that somewhere beneath the street, that water was flowing toward Alder Creek, past the plaza, past Site 4, carrying whatever it had collected along the way."],"answer":1,"evidence":"It was no longer only a question about a number; it was a question about a person she liked and respected, who was frightened, and who was asking her, without quite saying so, to share the weight of a decision he already felt guilty about."},{"type":"inference","q":"What realization makes Nadia stop walking at the end of the chapter?","options":["The library will close before midnight.","The Week 7 spike came after heavy rain.","Jonah has deleted other readings too."],"answer":1}],"grade":9,"words":769,"auto":{"fk":8.2,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g9-book-ch3","unit":"G9-U2","pool":"book","title":"After the Rain","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","book":"The Weight of Small Numbers","chapter":3,"text":"Nadia's older cousin Samira, who was twenty and home from college for the week, agreed to drive her to the creek on one condition: that Nadia explain, in complete sentences, why a reasonable person would want to stand in freezing mud at eight o'clock at night. Nadia explained on the way, and by the time they parked behind the Westgate plaza, Samira had stopped teasing and was holding the flashlight steady while Nadia knelt at the edge of the water.\n\nThe creek was high again, swollen and fast from the afternoon storm. Nadia filled a test tube at Site 4, exactly where she had filled one two weeks earlier, added the reagent and waited, counting under her breath, while the liquid slowly turned the same deep pink she had seen before.\n\nShe tested a second sample to be certain, and then, because a careful scientist would want a comparison, she walked upstream with Samira to Site 3, above the culvert. There the water turned only a pale, ordinary rose, the color it had been every week before the rain, which meant that the change was happening somewhere between the two sites, in the short stretch where the creek passed under the plaza's back lot.\n\nSamira swept the flashlight along the concrete wall of the culvert. Halfway down, a pipe jutted from the wall, and from it poured a steady stream of cloudy water with a faint, sour smell that made both of them wrinkle their noses.\n\n\"That's definitely not rainwater,\" Samira said slowly.\n\nNadia photographed the pipe twice, with the flashlight beam on it so the cloudy stream would be visible, and then recorded the time, the location and both readings in the notebook, her fingers stiff with cold. She did not know exactly what the pipe was or where it came from, but she understood now that the number in the spreadsheet had not been an accident. It had been a measurement, a true one, of something nobody had been looking for.\n\nThe next morning, before school, she found Jonah at his locker and showed him the photographs and the notebook. He read the page twice, and his expression moved through several stages, confusion, then disbelief, and then something that resembled anger.\n\n\"You went back without me,\" he said, and when she explained that there had been no time to ask because the rain was happening right then, he closed the notebook harder than he needed to. \"So now you want to change our entire project, the night before it's due, because of a pipe?\"\n\n\"I'm going to put back the real number, and I'm going to add the retest,\" Nadia said, her voice trembling slightly, though she kept going. \"If you want to argue that it's still an error, we can explain that on the poster too, but I refuse to turn in a chart that hides something we actually measured.\"\n\nFor a long moment, Jonah said nothing while the hallway filled with the noise of lockers and sneakers and a hundred conversations. Then he leaned back against the metal door and stared at the ceiling.\n\n\"You know what's annoying?\" he said finally. \"When I deleted that number, I told myself it was because the judges wouldn't understand it, but that wasn't really true. I deleted it because I didn't understand it; it didn't fit, it frightened me, and so I made it disappear.\"\n\nNadia waited.\n\n\"My dad is an accountant, and he always says that the numbers don't lie, and that the only way to get in trouble with numbers is to change them.\" He laughed, a short, tired laugh with no humor in it. \"He'd be pretty disappointed in me right now, and not because of any prize.\"\n\n\"So help me fix it,\" she said. \"We have until midnight.\"\n\nHe looked at the photographs again, at the cloudy water pouring from the pipe, and something in his face shifted, the way a picture changes when you finally recognize the second image hidden inside it.\n\n\"If the spike only happens after rain, then the pipe might be connected to something that overflows, which would explain why we never detected it on dry weeks,\" he said slowly, already pulling out his phone and opening the spreadsheet. \"We'd need a completely new chart that shows the rainy days separately.\"\n\nWhen she asked whether he could possibly make it in one afternoon, Jonah grinned for the first time all week and told her to watch him.","questions":[{"type":"why","q":"Why does Nadia also test the water at Site 3?","options":["to compare readings above and below the culvert","to check that Samira's flashlight still works","to collect extra samples for the poster display"],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best shows that Nadia now believes the spike was real?","options":["Nadia filled a test tube at Site 4, exactly where she had filled one two weeks earlier, added the reagent and waited, counting under her breath, while the liquid slowly turned the same deep pink she had seen before.","She did not know exactly what the pipe was or where it came from, but she understood now that the number in the spreadsheet had not been an accident.","He read the page twice, and his expression moved through several stages, confusion, then disbelief, and then something that resembled anger."],"answer":1,"evidence":"She did not know exactly what the pipe was or where it came from, but she understood now that the number in the spreadsheet had not been an accident."},{"type":"inference","q":"What does Jonah's confession at his locker reveal?","options":["He thought Nadia had invented the reading on purpose.","He had always planned to restore the data later.","He hid the number because it confused and scared him."],"answer":2},{"type":"theme","q":"How does the saying of Jonah's father help develop the story's theme?","options":["It suggests that honesty matters more than winning.","It shows that accountants make the best scientists.","It proves that his father wants him to quit science."],"answer":0}],"grade":9,"words":746,"auto":{"fk":9.1,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g9-book-ch4","unit":"G9-U2","pool":"book","title":"The Data That Didn't Fit","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","book":"The Weight of Small Numbers","chapter":4,"text":"The regional science fair filled the gymnasium of the community college with folding tables, humming laptops and the nervous energy of three hundred students in their most uncomfortable clothes. Nadia and Jonah's poster stood near the back, between a project on bridge designs and one about which brand of paper towel absorbed the most water.\n\nTheir poster looked different from the one they had imagined. The title had changed: it now read \"Does Heron Marsh Clean Alder Creek? (And What Happens After It Rains?)\" The original scissors chart was still there, but beside it sat a second graph, which Jonah had built in a single frantic afternoon. It separated the dry weeks from the rainy ones, and on the rainy weeks a sharp spike rose at Site 4 like a mountain on an otherwise flat map. Below it, Nadia had mounted the photograph of the pipe, along with a short paragraph that ended with a sentence she had rewritten a dozen times: \"We do not yet know the source of this discharge, but our data suggest it should be investigated.\"\n\nThe first two judges were polite and brief; they asked about sample sizes and the reliability of test kits, nodded, wrote on their clipboards and moved on. Nadia's stomach sank a little with each departure. Jonah, standing beside her in a borrowed tie, kept straightening the corners of the poster as if neatness alone might rescue them.\n\nThe third judge was different, a tall woman with gray hair cropped short, and her name tag identified her as an engineer with the county's water quality department. Ignoring the scissors chart entirely, she went straight to the photograph of the pipe and studied it for nearly a full minute in silence.\n\nWhen she asked where exactly the pipe was located, Nadia showed her on the map they had drawn, and the engineer continued, asking when the photograph had been taken, how many times they had tested the site, and whether they had seen the cloudy flow on dry days. Jonah answered the questions about the data, his voice steady, explaining how they had separated rainy weeks from dry ones and why the pattern pointed to the stretch of creek beneath the plaza.\n\n\"Would you mind,\" the engineer said finally, \"if I sent someone out to look at this pipe on Monday?\"\n\nNadia and Jonah did not win first place. That prize, and the summer research spot that came with it, went to a girl from another school who had designed a low-cost sensor for detecting lead in drinking water, a project so impressive that even Jonah admitted she deserved it. Their poster received a second-place ribbon and a handwritten note from the third judge: \"Excellent instinct. Never ignore the data that doesn't fit.\"\n\nTwo weeks later, an email arrived from the county. The inspection crew had traced the pipe to a restaurant at the back of the Westgate plaza. During a renovation years earlier, a drain that was supposed to connect to the sewer had been attached to the storm drain instead. On dry days, little flowed through it. During heavy rain, when the restaurant's floor drains and outdoor washing area overflowed, the wastewater poured straight into Alder Creek. The connection had existed for at least five years, and nobody had noticed.\n\nThe restaurant repaired the pipe within a month, and Ms. Okafor printed the email and taped it to the classroom door, where it stayed for the rest of the year.\n\nOn the last Saturday in May, Nadia and Jonah went back to the creek one more time, not for a project but simply to see. It had rained the night before. Nadia filled a test tube at Site 4, added the reagent and held it up to the light.\n\nThe liquid turned a pale, ordinary rose, which was clean enough, and Jonah studied it for a long time.\n\n\"It's funny,\" he said. \"That one number I deleted turned out to be the most important thing we measured.\"\n\n\"It almost wasn't measured at all,\" Nadia reminded him.\n\n\"I know,\" he said, picking up a flat stone and turning it over in his fingers. \"I keep thinking about how easy it was to change one cell in a spreadsheet, and how nobody would ever have known.\" He skipped the stone across the water, and it bounced three times before sinking. \"Except the creek.\"\n\nThey stood there a while longer, listening to the blackbirds in the reeds. Downstream, the marsh was turning green again, doing its slow, quiet work, and the water moved through it toward the river, carrying nothing that anyone needed to hide.","questions":[{"type":"craft","q":"Why does the author have the third judge ignore the scissors chart?","options":["to suggest that the judge dislikes very neat charts","to show that the unexpected data is what matters most","to reveal that the scissors chart contained a mistake"],"answer":1},{"type":"literal","q":"What did the county inspection find?","options":["a farm spilling fertilizer from its fields into the creek","a large crack in the main channel running through the marsh","a drain attached to the storm drain instead of the sewer"],"answer":2,"evidence":"During a renovation years earlier, a drain that was supposed to connect to the sewer had been attached to the storm drain instead."},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best shows how Jonah's view of the deleted number has changed?","options":["He skipped the stone across the water, and it bounced three times before sinking.","\"That one number I deleted turned out to be the most important thing we measured.\"","Jonah, standing beside her in a borrowed tie, kept straightening the corners of the poster as if neatness alone might rescue them."],"answer":1,"evidence":"\"That one number I deleted turned out to be the most important thing we measured.\""},{"type":"theme","q":"Which statement best expresses the theme of the whole story?","options":["Honest data can reveal truths that neat results hide.","Winning first place is the true goal of science.","Good friends should agree with each other's choices."],"answer":0}],"grade":9,"words":774,"auto":{"fk":8.1,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}}];
